<body>
<h1>Unit 19: Electric Charges</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">Electric charge is the first conserved quantity a student meets that is exactly conserved in every known interaction. The unit also contains one of the cleanest pieces of evidence in all of physics: charge is quantised, and the quantum is exact.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Charge is quantised: the elementary charge e = 1.602176634e-19 C exactly, and every observed charge in ordinary matter is an integer multiple of it.</li><li>Coulomb's law F = k q1 q2 / r^2 holds with k = 8.9875517923e9 N.m^2/C^2, and like charges repel while unlike charges attract.</li><li>Charge is conserved in every known interaction, and electric charge is an exact conserved quantum number in the Standard Model.</li><li>Charging by friction transfers electrons between surfaces; charging by induction moves electrons without any contact at all.</li><li>Conductors allow charge to move freely and insulators do not; in electrostatic equilibrium the field inside a conductor is zero and excess charge sits on the surface, concentrating at sharp points.</li><li>Earthing works because the Earth is an enormous conductor held at nearly constant potential.</li><li>Charge is quantised in units of e; the fractional charges seen in the fractional quantum Hall effect are a many-body property of a correlated electron liquid, not evidence that the electron is divisible.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Coulomb's law assumes static point charges in a uniform medium; extended bodies require integration and conductors require redistribution.</li><li>Charging by friction depends on the material pair, surface condition and humidity - the triboelectric order is a tendency, not a law.</li><li>The field inside a conductor is zero only in electrostatic equilibrium; a time-varying field drives currents inside it.</li><li>In semiconductors both electrons and holes carry current, so 'the charge carrier' model of a conductor has to be replaced by a two-carrier picture.</li><li>Coulomb's law is the static, vacuum result; for moving charges the magnetic field joins in, and an accelerating charge radiates, so the static formula alone no longer describes the system.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 4 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Electric charges</li><li>Charging by induction</li><li>Coulomb's law: force between two point charges</li><li>Force between multiple electric charges</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Charging by friction creates charge, so a charged body has more charge than it had before.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Friction only separates and transfers electrons that were already there. The total charge of the system is conserved, and each body ends up with an equal and opposite charge.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Rubbing transfers electrons from one surface to the other, so one body becomes negative and the other positive with equal magnitude. The total charge of the two-body system is unchanged; charge is conserved in every known interaction.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Two identical conducting spheres in contact are given a charge Q. When they are separated, each carries Q/2.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That is true only if the spheres are identical and share the charge equally. If the spheres differ in size, the smaller one takes a larger share because its potential rises faster for the same charge, and the system has a unique equilibrium.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For identical spheres, symmetry gives Q/2 each. For different spheres, the charge divides in proportion to their radii (for well-separated spheres), so the smaller sphere ends up with more than half the charge. The total is still Q.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Coulomb's law applies to any two charged objects, whatever their shape and size.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The law is exact for point charges, or for spherically symmetric charge distributions. For extended bodies the force must be found by integration, and the result depends on the geometry.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Coulomb's law F = k q1 q2 / r^2 holds for point charges in a uniform medium. For a uniformly charged spherical shell, the external field is exactly that of a point charge at the centre (the shell theorem) and the internal field is zero, so the law applies externally to spheres as well.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>Two point charges of +2.0 microcoulomb and -3.0 microcoulomb are placed 0.50 m apart in air. Find the magnitude and direction of the force between them, and the force on a 1.0 microcoulomb charge placed on the line joining them, 0.20 m from the +2.0 microcoulomb charge.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> F = k q1 q2 / r^2 = 8.99 x 10^9 x (2.0 x 10^-6)(3.0 x 10^-6) / 0.25 = 8.99 x 10^9 x 6.0 x 10^-12 / 0.25 = 0.216 N, attractive because the charges have opposite signs. For the third charge, the field of the +2.0 microcoulomb charge at 0.20 m is E1 = k q1 / r^2 = 8.99 x 10^9 x 2.0 x 10^-6 / 0.04 = 4.5 x 10^5 N/C, directed away from the + charge (towards the -3.0 microcoulomb charge, 0.30 m away). The field of the -3.0 microcoulomb charge at that point is E2 = 8.99 x 10^9 x 3.0 x 10^-6 / 0.09 = 3.0 x 10^5 N/C, directed towards the - charge, i.e. in the same direction as E1. Net E = 7.5 x 10^5 N/C, so the force on the 1.0 microcoulomb charge is F = q E = 0.75 N in the same direction (towards the negative charge).</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Use Gauss's law to show that the electric field outside a uniformly charged thin spherical shell of radius R and total charge Q is the same as that of a point charge Q at the centre, and that the field inside the shell is zero.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Draw a concentric Gaussian sphere of radius r &gt; R. By symmetry the field is radial and has the same magnitude everywhere on it, so the flux is E x 4 pi r^2. Gauss's law gives E x 4 pi r^2 = Q / epsilon0, so E = Q / (4 pi epsilon0 r^2) = k Q / r^2, exactly the field of a point charge at the centre. For r &lt; R the same Gaussian surface encloses no charge, so the flux is zero; by symmetry E must be zero, and the field inside a charged shell is indeed zero. The same argument with a cylindrical Gaussian surface gives the field of an infinite line charge, E = lambda / (2 pi epsilon0 r), and with a pillbox gives the field of an infinite charged plane, E = sigma / (2 epsilon0).</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>Two identical metal spheres on insulating handles are in contact, and a charge Q is transferred onto the pair. They are then separated without discharging. Explain what happens to the charge on each sphere, and what would change if the spheres were of different sizes.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> While the spheres touch they behave as one conductor, and the charge Q spreads over the combined surface until the whole is at one potential. When they are separated, each keeps whatever charge it had at that moment, and the total remains Q. For identical spheres the symmetry of the situation means each carries Q/2. If the spheres have different radii, the smaller one has a smaller capacitance per unit charge and its potential rises more steeply, so at equilibrium the smaller sphere carries more than half of the charge; for well-separated spheres the split is in proportion to the radii, and the charge flows from the larger to the smaller sphere as they are brought apart, because the smaller sphere's potential is then higher. The physics is the same in both cases: charge flows until the two spheres are at the same potential.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Elementary charge (exact)</td><td>1.602176634e-19 C</td></tr><tr><td>Coulomb constant</td><td>8.9875517923e9 N.m^2/C^2</td></tr><tr><td>Charge of one electron</td><td>-1.602176634e-19 C</td></tr><tr><td>Force between two protons 1 m apart</td><td>about 2.3e-28 N</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why is electric charge quantised, and can fractional charge ever be observed?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The Standard Model contains charge quantisation but does not derive it; quasiparticles with fractional charge do exist in certain condensed-matter states, which is a different phenomenon.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Is electric charge exactly conserved?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Experiments constrain any violation to an extraordinarily small level; neutron-decay searches continue to sharpen that bound.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Explain why a charged rod attracts a neutral paper strip, and draw the induced polarisation on the strip.</li><li>Predict the charge distribution on a hollow conducting sphere and on a sphere containing an off-centre cavity.</li><li>Compute how many electrons correspond to a charge of 1 nC and comment on what that says about the quantisation of charge.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/electric-charges">Read the chapter notes: Electric Charges</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-electro">Electrostatics theory lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-electrostatics">Electrostatics 3D lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units">NIST: SI Units</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-1-electric-charge">OpenStax: Electric Charge</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-3-coulombs-law">OpenStax: Coulomb's Law</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
